C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not survey the ways churches have been ordered and leave the choice to the reader. It says what the author believes Paul teaches when he writes about how the gathered Body is ordered, it gives the evidence from Paul’s own sentences, and it answers the strongest objections in the objectors’ own words."),
("p", "The position is this. Paul orders the assembly by separate instructions, and each instruction names its own ground. When he writes of gifts, the ground is that the Body is one and has many members (1 Corinthians 12:12–27). When he writes of the ascended Christ’s gifts to the church, the ground is the edifying of the body (Ephesians 4:12). When he writes of overseers and deacons, the ground is what such a man must be (1 Timothy 3:1–13; Titus 1:5–9). When he writes of the gathering at Corinth, the ground is that “God is not the author of confusion, but of peace” (1 Corinthians 14:33). When he writes of men and women, the grounds are headship and creation, “the law,” and Adam first formed and Eve deceived (1 Corinthians 11:3, 8–9; 14:34; 1 Timothy 2:13–14). When he writes of discipline, the grounds are a little leaven, a spirit to be saved, a brother to be ashamed and others to fear (1 Corinthians 5:5, 6; 2 Thessalonians 3:14; 1 Timothy 5:20). These are not one rule said in several places. Each is read as the passage states it."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, the volume asks the same four questions of every instruction: who is told, in what setting, what is to be done, and on what ground. Where a passage does not answer one of the four, the volume says so and does not borrow the answer from another passage. Third, where modern writers are named, they are named for what they say. A writer is quoted only from a text the author has read and that anyone can read for free. Otherwise the position is described in the author’s own words and no view is credited to a writer who has not been read."),
("p", "The volume is written for readers who know the passages and want to see them together. It does not require Greek. It does not take up the Lord’s Supper, which Study 72 reads, or the passages on praying in the Spirit and on tongues as prayer, which Study 81 reads. It reads 1 Corinthians 14 for the order of the gathering and cites the other studies where their ground begins. The reader is asked to keep a Bible open, to test every row of every table, and to correct the author where the text does."),
("p", "The author does not claim to have said the last word on how a church should be run. The claim is narrower. When each of Paul’s instructions is read for what it says and for the ground it gives, some orderings that are commonly defended with one verse turn out to rest on another, and some that are defended by custom turn out to be named by Paul. The reader will see which is which."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "Paul orders the gathered Body by separate instructions, each with its own stated ground, and the grounds are not one rule restated. Where Paul names no ground, the volume says so and builds none."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in four statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "The gathered Body is the church of God in a place, and Paul’s instructions to it each answer one question: gifts, offices, order in the meeting, men and women, or discipline.", "Chapters 1 to 8"),
    ("2", "Gifts are given to the Body by the Spirit and by the ascended Christ, and they are not the same list as the offices. Paul names two offices in the local assembly, overseer (called elder) and deacon.", "Chapters 3 to 7"),
    ("3", "In the meeting Paul gives order its own ground (edifying, and a God of peace) and gives men and women instructions whose grounds are headship, creation and the law, not local custom alone.", "Chapters 9 to 12"),
    ("4", "Discipline is ordered by stated aims: purity, a spirit saved, shame, restoration, and warning. Matthew 18 is left in its Kingdom setting.", "Chapters 13 and 14"),
  ], [0.15, 0.57, 0.28]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Take any two of Paul’s instructions and ask whether they give the same ground. If they do, the instructions may be one rule said twice and the thesis fails. If they give different grounds, the thesis stands, and the reader will have seen why Paul never gives the assembly a single rule for its order."),
]
